Cache consistency mantra: ensure read-your-writes and predictable invalidation. After any local update, the value layer must reflect the pending value immediately (without a network read), and TTL caches should be updated on write (write-through) so short-lived caches never serve stale data. Keep one memoized values object per entity so its TTL cache is actually effective. Make batched update behavior match the non-batched path (e.g., text coercion) to avoid divergent semantics. Invalidate or refresh caches after structural changes (e.g., creating a column clears text-column caches). Narrow reads to only what’s needed (pass column_ids; prefer typed fields like linked_item_ids) to reduce latency and staleness windows.
When reviewing or testing, ask: “If I write, then immediately read, do I get the new value without an extra call? If I change structure, where is cache invalidated? If TTL=0, do I always fetch fresh? If TTL>0, does a write update the cache?” Verify subitem reads are column-scoped, item loads include required context (e.g., include_board), and connect-boards decode uses typed fields. Run the same flows with TTL on/off and with/without batching to catch timing bugs, and assert that immediate reads after commits and within transactions never surface out-of-date values.
